Subject: [PATCH] webgpu: plain-atomics fallback for the Forward+ cluster builder

Forward+ (clustered) could not run under WebGPU at all, for two reasons that
both live in the cluster builder's fragment shader.

1. Subgroup operations. cluster_render.glsl elects one writer per cluster using
   subgroupBroadcastFirst / subgroupBallot / subgroupBallotExclusiveBitCount /
   subgroupOr. WebGPU exposes no subgroup support whatsoever -- the driver
   already reports LIMIT_SUBGROUP_* as 0 -- but nothing in servers/rendering
   ever read those limits, so the shader was compiled unconditionally and the
   cluster build pass could never be created.

2. gl_HelperInvocation. The election is guarded by that builtin, and Tint's
   SPIR-V reader aborts on it outright:

     spirv/reader/parser/parser.cc:670 internal compiler error:
     TINT_UNIMPLEMENTED unhandled SPIR-V BuiltIn: HelperInvocation (val = 23)

   That is the same failure mode as the Volatile decoration fixed in 0009: a
   TINT_UNIMPLEMENTED abort is a wasm trap in the browser, i.e. a frozen page,
   not a graceful "effect unavailable". cluster_render.glsl is the only live
   user of gl_HelperInvocation in the engine (the other reference, in
   scene_forward_lights_inc.glsl, sits inside `#if 0`), which is why Forward
   Mobile was unaffected and this stayed hidden.

The fix adds a USE_SUBGROUPS variant pair and lets a driver without
fragment-stage subgroup ballot+arithmetic select the plain-atomics path, which
produces an identical cluster buffer:

  - Every invocation the ballot would have grouped shares the same
    cluster_offset (that is what the ballot groups on) and the same flat
    element_index, so they all atomicOr the very same word and the very same
    bit. atomicOr is idempotent, so dropping the election and letting each
    invocation write is bit-identical. The election exists only to cut atomic
    traffic; it is an optimisation, not semantics.
  - Likewise subgroupOr on the depth bits: OR-merging across the subgroup and
    writing once yields the same final word as each invocation OR-ing its own
    bit into its own offset.
  - The fallback also skips the gl_HelperInvocation test. Letting helper
    invocations write is conservative -- a cluster may list an element that
    barely misses it, costing a little shading work, never dropping one. Godot
    already ships this same trade-off via sc_use_helper_check on Apple/MSAA.

The two new variants are APPENDED to ShaderVariant, so every driver that does
report subgroup support keeps compiling exactly the shader it did before and
existing platforms are untouched.

Verified offline, GPU-free, with the vendored-glslang + Tint harness at the
engine's real target env (Vulkan 1.1 / SPIR-V 1.3): cluster_render.glsl
translates to valid WGSL in both the `default` and `attachment` (non-subgroup)
variants, where previously it could not even be compiled to SPIR-V. Not yet
verified on hardware.

diff --git a/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.cpp b/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.cpp
index 1df4b6054d..3bc79c94aa 100644
--- a/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.cpp
+++ b/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.cpp
@@ -56,22 +56,36 @@ ClusterBuilderSharedDataRD::ClusterBuilderSharedDataRD() {
 		ms.sample_count = RD::TEXTURE_SAMPLES_4;
 
 		Vector<String> variants;
+		variants.push_back("\n#define USE_SUBGROUPS\n");
+		variants.push_back("\n#define USE_ATTACHMENT\n#define USE_SUBGROUPS\n");
 		variants.push_back("");
 		variants.push_back("\n#define USE_ATTACHMENT\n");
 
 		ClusterRender::ShaderVariant shader_variant;
 		RenderingDevice *rd = RD::get_singleton();
+
+		// The cluster builder's fragment shader uses subgroup ballot + arithmetic to
+		// elect one writer per cluster. Drivers that expose no subgroup support in the
+		// fragment stage (WebGPU exposes none at all) need the plain-atomics fallback,
+		// which is functionally identical -- see cluster_render.glsl.
+		const uint64_t subgroup_stages = rd->limit_get(RD::LIMIT_SUBGROUP_IN_SHADERS);
+		const uint64_t subgroup_ops = rd->limit_get(RD::LIMIT_SUBGROUP_OPERATIONS);
+		const bool subgroups_usable =
+				(subgroup_stages & RD::SHADER_STAGE_FRAGMENT_BIT) &&
+				(subgroup_ops & RD::SUBGROUP_BALLOT_BIT) &&
+				(subgroup_ops & RD::SUBGROUP_ARITHMETIC_BIT);
+
 		if (rd->has_feature(RD::SUPPORTS_FRAGMENT_SHADER_WITH_ONLY_SIDE_EFFECTS)) {
 			fb_format = rd->framebuffer_format_create_empty();
 			blend_state = RD::PipelineColorBlendState::create_disabled();
-			shader_variant = ClusterRender::SHADER_NORMAL;
+			shader_variant = subgroups_usable ? ClusterRender::SHADER_NORMAL : ClusterRender::SHADER_NORMAL_NO_SUBGROUPS;
 		} else {
 			Vector<RD::AttachmentFormat> afs;
 			afs.push_back(RD::AttachmentFormat());
 			afs.write[0].usage_flags = RD::TEXTURE_USAGE_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_BIT;
 			fb_format = rd->framebuffer_format_create(afs);
 			blend_state = RD::PipelineColorBlendState::create_blend();
-			shader_variant = ClusterRender::SHADER_USE_ATTACHMENT;
+			shader_variant = subgroups_usable ? ClusterRender::SHADER_USE_ATTACHMENT : ClusterRender::SHADER_USE_ATTACHMENT_NO_SUBGROUPS;
 		}
 
 		cluster_render.cluster_render_shader.initialize(variants);
diff --git a/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.h b/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.h
index 49b039a997..9bb1ce1f60 100644
--- a/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.h
+++ b/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/cluster_builder_rd.h
@@ -73,9 +73,14 @@ class ClusterBuilderSharedDataRD {
 		RID shader_version;
 		RID shader;
 
+		// The _NO_SUBGROUPS variants are appended, not inserted, so the existing two
+		// keep their indices and every driver that reports subgroup support keeps
+		// compiling exactly the shader it did before.
 		enum ShaderVariant {
 			SHADER_NORMAL,
 			SHADER_USE_ATTACHMENT,
+			SHADER_NORMAL_NO_SUBGROUPS,
+			SHADER_USE_ATTACHMENT_NO_SUBGROUPS,
 		};
 
 		enum PipelineVersion {
diff --git a/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/shaders/cluster_render.glsl b/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/shaders/cluster_render.glsl
index 126bd75f75..1662b5a54c 100644
--- a/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/shaders/cluster_render.glsl
+++ b/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/shaders/cluster_render.glsl
@@ -65,9 +65,11 @@ void main() {
 
 #VERSION_DEFINES
 
+#ifdef USE_SUBGROUPS
 #extension GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_ballot : enable
 #extension GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_arithmetic : enable
 #extension GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_vote : enable
+#endif
 
 // On Apple platforms, gl_HelperInvocation (simd_is_helper_thread()) is unreliable with MSAA, causing rendering artifacts.
 // Setting this to false will disable the helper invocation check.
@@ -117,6 +119,16 @@ void main() {
 
 	uint aux = 0;
 
+	// Without subgroup ops (e.g. WebGPU, which exposes none), every invocation writes
+	// its own bit instead of electing one writer per cluster. This is bit-identical:
+	// all invocations the ballot would have grouped share the same cluster_offset and
+	// the same flat element_index, so they all atomicOr the very same word and bit, and
+	// atomicOr is idempotent. The election exists only to cut atomic traffic, not to
+	// change the result. gl_HelperInvocation is also skipped -- Tint's SPIR-V reader
+	// aborts on that builtin, and letting helper invocations write is conservative
+	// (a cluster may list an element that barely misses it, costing a little shading
+	// work but never dropping one).
+#ifdef USE_SUBGROUPS
 	uint cluster_thread_group_index;
 	if (!sc_use_helper_check || !gl_HelperInvocation) {
 		//https://advances.realtimerendering.com/s2017/2017_Sig_Improved_Culling_final.pdf
@@ -142,6 +154,9 @@ void main() {
 			aux = atomicOr(cluster_render.data[usage_write_offset], usage_write_bit);
 		}
 	}
+#else
+	aux = atomicOr(cluster_render.data[usage_write_offset], usage_write_bit);
+#endif
 
 	//find the current element in the depth usage list and mark the current depth as used
 	float unit_depth = depth_interp * state.inv_z_far;
@@ -151,12 +166,18 @@ void main() {
 	uint z_write_offset = cluster_offset + state.cluster_depth_offset + element_index;
 	uint z_write_bit = 1 << z_bit;
 
+#ifdef USE_SUBGROUPS
 	if (!sc_use_helper_check || !gl_HelperInvocation) {
 		z_write_bit = subgroupOr(z_write_bit); //merge all Zs
 		if (cluster_thread_group_index == 0) {
 			aux = atomicOr(cluster_render.data[z_write_offset], z_write_bit);
 		}
 	}
+#else
+	// OR-merging the subgroup's depth bits and writing once is the same final word as
+	// each invocation OR-ing its own bit into its own offset, which is what this does.
+	aux = atomicOr(cluster_render.data[z_write_offset], z_write_bit);
+#endif
 
 #ifdef USE_ATTACHMENT
 	frag_color = vec4(float(aux));
